== Key debates ==

=== The role of confederal authority ===
{{Main|Cantonal sovereignty amendment}}

{{See also|Confederal Question|Special Period|Confederal emergency}}

The appropriate scope of the competencies of the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] and coordination remains an issue. Anti-statist platforms argue that confederal mechanisms should be limited to voluntary coordination, with full authority retained at the [[Communes of Balisca|commune]] and cantonal levels. Statist platforms argue that effective response to [[W:Climate collapse|ecological crisis]], economic coordination, and [[Social protection in Balisca|social protection]] requires stronger binding authority at the confederal level.

The debate re-emerged following the [[W:Climate emergency declaration|declaration of an indefinite climate emergency]] in 1985 by the administration of [[Diego Navarro]]. Under a confederal emergency, the confederal government is authorized to centralize power (most notably the [[Confederal Armed Forces|Armed Forces]]) and override cantonal sovereignty in order to defend the interests of the confederation from external threat. Under the succeeding administrations, there has been a growth in confederal re-armament unrelated to the climate emergency, and (overall benevolent) confederal action regarding internal issues. This growing centralization has prompted fears among autonomist platforms that this climate emergency could be used to indefinitely centralize power and override cantonal sovereignty. The 2021 unrest exposed coordination failures in housing policy and economic response. Some interpreted the crisis as evidence of insufficient confederal capacity; others interpreted it as evidence that centralized institutions had become unresponsive to local conditions.

=== Technology and transformation ===

The role of technology in social transformation generates significant debate. [[Accelerationism|Accelerationist]] platforms argue that technological development should be embraced as a means toward [[W:Communization|communization]] and post-scarcity. [[W:Degrowth|Degrowth]] advocates express caution regarding technological optimism, arguing that material limits cannot be transcended through technical means. [[W:Bright green environmentalism|Bright green]] advocates argue for a middle position, embracing technological solutions while maintaining ecological commitment.

Particular debate has emerged regarding [[W:Automation|automation]], [[W:Artificial intelligence|artificial intelligence]] (especially since the [[W:AI boom|AI boom]]), and their implications for labor and distribution. Accelerationists advocate for accelerated automation as a means to reduce necessary labor time; critics argue that automation without appropriate governance could increase inequality and undermine working-class power.

=== Citizenship and belonging ===
{{Main|Baliscan nationality law}}

The [[Constitution of Balisca|Baliscan Constitution of 2022]] expanded pathways to citizenship and established new protections for non-citizens, but debates continue regarding the appropriate scope of citizenship and belonging. [[W:Cosmopolitanism|Cosmopolitan]] platforms advocate for reduced barriers to citizenship, expanded rights for non-citizens, and deeper integration with global solidarity networks. [[W:Civic nationalism|Nationalist]] platforms advocate for strengthened conditions for citizenship and expanded cultural integration requirements.

The status of long-term residents who are not citizens—particularly in urban areas with high immigrant populations—remains contested. Non-citizens face exclusion from cooperative housing and certain economic participation; some platforms argue for eliminating these barriers, while others argue that maintaining citizen privileges preserves the commitments that sustain Baliscan democracy.

